 "post": {
  "title": "You'd Defend a Choice You Never Made #shorts",
  "description": "Ever defended a pick you didn't make? Tell me in the comments. Send this to the friend who's never wrong. Otto picks the red logo, certain. Lila swaps it. In 'Failure to Detect Mismatches Between Intention and Outcome in a Simple Decision Task' (Science, 2005), Petter Johansson, Lars Hall, Sverker Sikström and Andreas Olsson showed 120 people pairs of faces and asked which was more attractive. On some trials, by sleight of hand, they handed back the face the person had NOT chosen and asked why they preferred it. Only 13% of these swaps were detected right away, and people went on to explain their 'choice'. It's called choice blindness. #psychology #shorts #choiceblindness #decisions #overthinking",
  "tags": [
   "choice blindness",
   "decision making",
   "overthinking",
   "Johansson and Hall",
   "psychology"
  ]
